WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.533
Following a

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:02.035
tumultuous month for banks

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.570
worldwide during the 2008

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.905
financial crisis, it was Jamie Dimon,

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.906
along with the other bankers

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.942
that really steered

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.444
this economy through what we saw.

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:13.580
He led the rescue of Bear Stearns

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.382
and Washington Mutual,

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:15.849
helped avert

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.217
a total collapse of the country's

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:18.151
financial system.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:19.519
So I asked him

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.654
why did we just see two more banks fail?

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.890
Could it have been avoided

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.926
What we do know is that

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:29.396
regulators told Silicon Valley Bank

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.264
at least six times,

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:32.465
you've got a problem

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:33.600
and your vulnerabilities,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.834
you need to fix them.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:35.769
Because of a law change.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:36.436
Four years ago,

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.470
they didn't have to fix them.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:37.771
They didn't.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.572
That was just a recommendation.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:41.374
They didn't fix those vulnerabilities.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.108
I don't think it has to do

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:43.143
with law change.

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:44.210
They should have fixed it.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.012
The regulation forced it. Yes.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:47.113
So let me ask you this question,

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.382
because the regulators can't force it.

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:51.284
Should the public know

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:52.285
when banks like that

00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:54.421
are warned multiple times?

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.755
Or is the risk too great

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.090
that then there's a run on the bank?

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.925
If you wave a flag and say,

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.326
this bank has an issue.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.062
So you may know more than I do about it

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:02.662
because I didn't read

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.330
all those regulatory reports down.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.498
But my view is

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:07.167
the regulators could have forced it.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.802
If my regulators called me up

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:09.169
and said,

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:10.437
we do not like X

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:11.771
and you have to change it,

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:12.972
I would change it.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:13.773
You would

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.075
There was no law change

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.743
that made that not possible.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.712
There is a 20, 21 regulation change

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:20.880
that would said that

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.881
this was just guidance.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.184
So you're saying it wasn't just guidance?

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:25.218
I have just guidance, too.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:26.386
But when they told me I have to do it,

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:27.654
I will do it. I'm doing it, yes.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.155
So let's talk about regulation.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:29.789
You say better

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.791
regulation would not have prevented.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.327
No, no, it would have. So what's better?

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:35.628
I think, again,

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.031
we have a liquidity discount Windows.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.365
We have mortgage laws.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:40.500
We've got capital laws.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.601
We've got TLAC laws.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:42.268
We've got

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.703
how you can use the discount window.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.438
There are 100 rules.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:46.306
I'm saying

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.740
thoughtfully adjust

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.442
the mix of how you do those.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.743
Make a bit of changes

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.011
if you have uninsured deposits.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.746
But that means held to maturity.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.248
Should you have debt issued,

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:56.683
which are the supervisors

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:57.283
for something

00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.453
you're not statutorily, not voluntarily.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.255
And I think you can make a lot of change

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.456
or improve the system.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:04.791
But that's always true in any system.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:05.992
I've never seen it not be true.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:07.060
Write in your annual letter

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:07.460
about what

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.162
you call your word Whack-A-Mole.

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:10.029
You seem to like that

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.231
word and politically

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:12.332
motivated responses

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.300
to the banking crisis. Like what?

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:14.601
Well, no.

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:15.435
I think
very often

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.203
when it comes to regulations you have

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.071
this is the problem.

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:19.005
They try to fix that,

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:21.074
but it often is unintended consequences.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.576
If you want healthy regional banks,

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:24.377
particularly banks.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:25.745
You should also listen to them

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.413
about what excess cost us

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:28.882
their ability to compete.

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:30.016
You know, what extra

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:31.551
high cost debt does to it

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:33.853
and and have a thoughtful conversation

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.555
about what are the things we can do to

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:36.789
to make it easier for you

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.024
and make the system safer.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.492
I believe that that's doable.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.294
It's not doable without the conversation

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:42.228
and without the analysis.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:43.830
I was reading some things

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.031
you said in 2008,

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:46.533
and this is in the middle of the crisis.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:47.200
You just acquired

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.735
Bear Stearns in a fire sale

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.103
trying to stabilize the banking system.

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:50.970
And then you said

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.405
if you don't worry in this business,

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.373
you're crazy.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.908
What are you most worried about right now

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:56.242
in these two things?

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:57.610
Well, I think all this

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.978
quantitative tightening,

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:01.047
the effect it has on long term rates,

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:02.549
inflation, higher for longer.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:04.284
That's kind of tied going out.

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:06.452
So even when Bear Stearns went down,

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.354
you think other people have seen that

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:08.721
and said,

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:09.722
I have too much leverage,

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.357
too much mortgage debt, too much this

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.659
and made some changes.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.327
Lehman went down six months later.

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:15.295
I think they might have been able

00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:16.262
to fix that in the meantime.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:17.263
So that's a little bit

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.531
of a warning to people.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.433
But I think this war in Ukraine, well,

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.202
I think that is changing

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:24.237
everything we think about the world,

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:25.171
how we should think about

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.973
safety and security,

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:29.075
even around food, energy.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:31.010
It's changing economic relationship.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:31.477
Is right,

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.513
really in the relation with us and China.

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.348
That is the most important thing.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:37.150
I mean, if you want to have a free

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.985
and democratic Western world

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:40.053
and an American century

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.221
in the next hundred century,

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:42.655
that's what we've got to focus on.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.490
You said last year on that front,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.858
the autocratic world thinks

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.860
that the Western world is a little lazy

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:49.229
and incompetent

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:49.562
and there's

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:50.964
a little bit of truth to that.

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:52.131
Is that still how you see it?

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:52.631
Yeah,

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:53.633
I think, you know,

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:54.200
I think we do

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:54.767
a lot of things

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.803
that we're in great position.

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:58.338
So, you know, for the American public,

00:03:58.338 --> 00:03:59.405
we have food,

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.506
water, energy,

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:01.741
with the most prosperous nation

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.610
the world's ever seen. That's still true.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.744
The most innovative.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:05.878
Just go to Atlanta,

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.347
go to not some people.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:08.281
These are Silicon Valley,

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:09.082
but in L.A., too.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:10.783
It's in New York. It's in Brooklyn.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.151
You know, it's all over the place.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:13.920
We have the GDP

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.622
per person is $70,000

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:17.690
plus Chinese is 15,000.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:18.858
They import

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:20.760
10 million barrels of oil a day.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.295
They don't have

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:23.463
the Atlantic in the Pacific.

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.731
They have a very complicated

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:25.798
neighborhood.

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:26.599
You know, they're angry.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:27.166
Their neighbors, we're

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.368
all starting to rearm.

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:30.536
We've got a great hand on the other hand.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:31.904
We haven't done a particularly good job

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:32.972
taking care of our lower paid

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:35.074
citizens or inner city schools,

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:37.143
immigration, taxation.

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:39.212
I think people should pay their taxes.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:40.413
Litigation which, you know,

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:42.215
we of course, 1% of GDP.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:42.882
And if it was

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.550
if it created more fairness in life,

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.585
I'd say I was good.

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:48.221
But is capricious, arbitrary, slow,

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:49.322
you know,

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:50.556
and so I can go on and on

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.058
about mortgages affordable?

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.294
You worry about affordable housing.

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:55.328
You know, we all agree with that.

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:56.796
But very often the problem is affordable

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:58.197
housing isn't capital.

00:04:58.197 --> 00:04:59.766
It's local zoning. Laws.

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:00.667
So if America

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:01.934
just got out of its own way,

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:03.670
I think we can grow 3% a year.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.505
Are you worried about how close

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.674
Iran, Russia and China

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:08.641
are to one another?

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.176
Right now and are getting to one another?

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:11.110
Do you think about that?

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:12.612
You're a student of history.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.147
I mean, if you were going to ally

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:15.581
with the Western world,

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.250
America and Europe

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.151
and Western World

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.552
War you're going to ally with

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:21.287
Iran and Russia. I mean, please.

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:22.021
Am I worried?

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:23.656
Not really. That doesn't worry you.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:24.223
No worries.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:26.259
Some of our top generals. Okay.

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:27.360
You said military. Yes.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.961
You should worry about Japan.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:31.130
But I just told you what a great hand

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.666
America has seen with kind of Europe.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:35.201
What we should be doing

00:05:35.201 --> 00:05:36.369
is our making sure

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:38.171
we're the allies, not just militarily

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.205
but economically,

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.174
strategically, diplomacy,

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.709
development, finance.

00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:43.209
You know,

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.610
we have the rest of the world

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:45.278
want to join us.

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:46.279
So if you're India,

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:48.181
would you rather be part of that group?

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:49.248
A part of the Western world?

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:50.283
Are we doing enough

00:05:50.283 --> 00:05:52.051
to entice or not enough? No.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:52.585
I think,

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:53.086
you know, Bob

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:53.853
Gates in his book,

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:56.155
I actually called The Symphony of Powers.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:56.656
I like that

00:05:56.656 --> 00:05:58.758
said the Symphony includes diplomacy,

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.460
development, finance,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.294
you having business

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.495
involved in helping develop,

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:03.229
you know, Africa,

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:04.931
Latin America, parts of Asia.

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:06.599
I think we could do a far better job

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:08.434
courting that as a policy.

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:09.001
I think, by the way,

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.870
the administration thinks that, too.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.038
And the other thing,

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.639
you can't take trade off the table.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:14.741
So the first thing we do

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:15.975
is we take trade off the table

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:16.843
from the start.

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:18.611
No trade may be the most important thing

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:19.579
for these countries.

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:20.580
And while there are legitimate

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.848
complaints about trade,

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:24.150
we can do it better. We can do it right.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:24.984
All right.

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:26.386
And we should be very thoughtful

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:27.120
about it.

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:28.621
Should the FDIC insure

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:30.823
more than $250,000?

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:31.624
Because I think

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:32.592
a lot of people feel right

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:34.360
now, after what we've seen, that

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.396
every deposit is just insured

00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:37.897
with no ceiling.

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:38.998
You know, I think probably.

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:39.866
But it goes back to my issue

00:06:39.866 --> 00:06:41.067
about regulations.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.101
If you do this,

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:43.102
you don't need to do that.

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:43.870
If you do this,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:44.704
you don't need to do that.

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.272
If you do them both, you can hurt

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:47.407
So just be very thoughtful.

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:50.143
We probably we should raise the deposit.

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:50.710
I don't know yet.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:51.210
I mean, again,

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.445
you got to do the analysis in that.

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:52.979
What about

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:54.247
I think that would help community banks.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:55.281
It would help raise it.

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:56.816
Okay. So final question on that.

00:06:56.816 --> 00:06:59.252
Should the banks like yours, the big guys

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.187
with the most assets

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:02.755
that have an implicit guarantee

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:03.523
that you're essentially

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:04.157
not going to fail,

00:07:04.157 --> 00:07:05.425
pay a higher percentage

00:07:05.425 --> 00:07:07.193
in the deposit insurance?

00:07:07.193 --> 00:07:08.728
Oh, God, I don't know, aggressive.

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:09.829
I'll leave that to everybody.

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:10.897
You you've been on that?

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:12.231
No. You guys should pay more.

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:13.266
If we were too big to fail,

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:13.599
by the way,

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:14.300
our bonds wouldn't

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:15.334
trade the way they tried

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:16.068
Just so you know.

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.137
And you were in the markets and so.

00:07:18.137 --> 00:07:19.472
Okay, talk about the economy.

00:07:19.472 --> 00:07:20.273
You wrote a lot about this

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.340
in your annual letter.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.575
Has this banking crisis,

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:23.676
even though you think it's almost over,

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.111
which I'm really glad to hear

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:25.978
though, increase

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.047
chances of a recession here. Yes.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.983
But I look at like it's not definitive.

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:31.851
It's just like

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:33.686
another weight on the scale.

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:34.153
And think of it

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:34.454
as you know,

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:35.555
people are just like raising

00:07:35.555 --> 00:07:36.889
rates on a 50 basis points

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:38.157
or something like that.

00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:39.792
We are seeing people reduce

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:40.660
lending a little bit,

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:41.594
cut back a little bit,

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:42.662
pull back a little bit.

00:07:42.662 --> 00:07:44.730
It won't necessarily force a recession,

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.199
but it is recessionary

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:50.069
recession more likely

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.571
